  "query_preview": "..\nTonight-Sunday: The deep low spiraling off the WA Coast will \nbecome stretched and slowly limp through the region. Mid and \nhigh clouds will continue to stream inland ahead of the low with\nperiods of sprinkles and/or light rain showers Saturday evening\nthrough Sunday as the remnants of the system passes through. As\nmentioned in the previous discussion, rain probabilities for \nSaturday evening 5-11PM have come down quite a bit given the \nweakening nature of the passing wave. Precipitation chances vary\nanywhere from 5-30% for North Idaho and the eastern third of WA\nwith mean QPF amounts between a trace and 0.02\". This leads to \nincreasing confidence for very light precipitation if any at all\nfor most of the trick-or-treaters in these areas. Central WA \nwill remain dry. Rain chances will increase closer to 40-70% for\nextreme southeastern WA around Asotin County and into the Camas\nPrairie and lower Idaho Palouse Saturday night with a 20% \nchance for picking up a wetting rain (0.10\"). Winds through the \nperiod will be light from the north and northeast through Sunday\nmorning then shift around to the west and northwest. For the \nmost part, speeds will be less than 10 mph though some localized\nchanneling tonight and Saturday morning will lead to winds of \n10-15 mph for the Okanogan Valley and Purcell Trench of North \nIdaho.\n\nMonday-Wednesday: A ridge of high pressure builds into the region \nstarting Sunday night, anchors overhead on Monday then begins \nto receive some blows from Pacific waves on Tuesday and \nWednesday. This is shaping up to be a dry period for most areas \nof the Inland NW. There are several variations of the waves \ncoming into the ridge Tuesday and Wednesday which has some \npotential to bring passing mountain showers but little in the \nway of impacts or appreciable precipitation and NBM PoPs are \naround 15% or less. Temperatures will remain close to seasonal \naverages or slightly warmer. The greatest weather impact will \nlikely be areas of dense morning fog impacting travel each \nmorning commute. \n\nThursday-Sunday: Ensembles are in fairly good agreement of a \ndeeper Pacific trough swinging toward the PacNW coastline \nduring this time but evolution of this system inland comes in \nmany variations. This leads to lower confidence regarding the \ndetails of the forecast for this period. 40% of the solutions \nindicate widespread precipitation while 60% are dry. There is \nalso a lot of uncertainty with the progression of this through \nthe region and whether this will deliver windy conditions or \nnot. At this time, there is moderate to high confidence for \nanother cool trough to settle toward the PacNW Coastline but \nmoderate to high uncertainty when precipitation will return to \nthe Inland NW, precise snow levels, and how this could impact \nwinds. Stay tuned. /sb",
